==English==
[[Image:A_full_moon_reflecting_off_the_river_-_NOAA.jpg|thumb|Moonlight]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|monelight}}, from {{inh|en|ang|[[mōna|mōnan]] [[lēoht]]|t=moonlight|lit=moon's light, light of the moon}}. Equivalent to {{af|en|moon|light}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|munelicht}} ~ {{m|sco|muinlicht}}, {{cog|fy|moanneljocht}}, {{cog|nl|maanlicht}}, {{cog|de|Mondlicht}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|mo͞on'līt}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈmunˌlaɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-moonlight.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|moon|light}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|sometimes|attributive}} The [[light#Noun|light]] [[reflect]]ed from the [[Moon]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|II|1|text=If you will patiently dance in our round<br>And see our '''moonlight''' revels, go with us;<br>If not, shun me, and I will spare your haunts.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|V|1|text=How sweet the '''moonlight''' sleeps upon this bank!<br>Here will we sit and let the sounds of music<br>Creep in our ears: soft stillness and the night<br>Become the touches of sweet harmony.}}
#* {{RQ:Smollett Peregrine Pickle|24|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/4084/4084-h/4084-h.htm|text={{...}} the sight of the blade which glistened by '''moonlight''' in his face, checked, in some sort, the ardour of his assailant {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1798|author={{w|William Wordsworth}}|title=The Idiot Boy|lines=1–4|url=http://www.bartleby.com/145/ww137.html
|passage=’Tis eight o’clock,—a clear March night,<br>The moon is up,—the sky is blue,<br>The owlet, in the '''moonlight''' air,<br>Shouts from nobody knows where;}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1830|author={{w|Oliver Wendell Holmes}}|title=Ballad of the Oysterman|lines=5–6|url=http://www.bartleby.com/380/poem/43.html
|passage=It was the pensive oysterman that saw a lovely maid,<br>Upon a '''moonlight''' evening, a-sitting in the shade;}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Shirley|chapter=13|passage=She passed away noiselessly, and the '''moonlight''' kissed the wall which her shadow had dimmed.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1889|author={{w|Robert Louis Stevenson}}|title={{w|The Master of Ballantrae}}|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/864/864-h/864-h.htm|chapter=12
|passage=“{{...}} What say you, gentlemen, shall we have a '''moonlight''' hunt?”}}
#* {{RQ:Stoker Dracula|3|text=The windows were curtainless, and the yellow '''moonlight''', flooding in through the diamond panes, enabled one to see even colours, whilst it softened the wealth of dust which lay over all and disguised in some measure the ravages of time and the moth.}}
#* {{RQ:Fitzgerald Great Gatsby|year=1953|6|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=uRVGCgAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false|text=They were still under the white plum tree and their faces were touching except for a pale thin ray of '''moonlight''' between.}}
#* {{RQ:Tolkien Hobbit|16|272|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=hFfhrCWiLSMC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false|text=It was as if a globe had been filled with '''moonlight''' and hung before them in a net woven of the glint of frosty stars.}}
#* '''1957''', {{w|Sylvia Dee}}, “{{w|Moonlight Swim}}” (song recorded by {{w|Nick Noble}} and {{w|Elvis Presley}}),<sup>[http://www.azlyrics.com/lyrics/elvispresley/moonlightswim.html]</sup>
#*: Let’s go on a '''moonlight''' swim<br>Far away from the crowd<br>All alone upon the beach<br>Our lips and our arms<br>Close within each other’s reach<br>Will be on a '''moonlight''' swim
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1958|author={{w|Chinua Achebe}}|title={{w|Things Fall Apart}}|chapter=2|publisher=William Heinemann|location=London
|passage=On a '''moonlight''' night it would be different. The happy voices of children playing in open fields would then be heard. And perhaps those not so young would be playing in pairs in less open places, and old men and women would remember their youth.}}

