===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[work]] [[on the side]] (at a secondary job), often in the [[evening]] or during the [[night]].
#* {{quote-av|en|passage=There are three individual rear seats. They all slide, they all fold, or they can all be removed completely, so that you can '''moonlight''' as a van.|year=2004|month=July|actor={{w|James May|James D. May}}|title={{w|Top Gear (2002 TV series)}}|oclc=1048213736|season=4|number=7|episode=MPVs as minicabs|time=00:22:29|writers={{w|Richard Porter}}; Paul Kerensa|network={{w|BBC Two}}|location=United Kingdom; {{w|Isle of Man}}; Channel Islands}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title={{w|The Sixth Extinction: An Unnatural History}}|year=2014|author={{w|Elizabeth Kolbert}}|publisher=Picador|ISBN=9781250062185|passage=Believing the bones to belong to a cave bear, the quarry owner passed them on to a local schoolteacher, Johann Carl Fuhlrott, who '''moonlighted''' as a fossilist.|page=240}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} To engage in an activity other than what one is known for.
#{{lb|en|by extension|of an|inanimate object}} To perform a secondary function substantially different from its supposed primary function, as in [[wikipedia:Protein_moonlighting|protein moonlighting]].
# {{lb|en|British|dated}} To carry out [[undeclared work]].

====Usage notes====
In American English, to moonlight is simply to work at secondary employment;<ref>Mish, Drederick C. (ed.). 1995. ''Merriam-Webster's Collegiate Dictionary.'' 10th ed. Springfield, MA: Merriam-Webster.</ref> in British English, it used to imply working secretly (i.e. not paying tax on the extra money earned), but more recent editions of some UK dictionaries no longer differentiate between the US and UK meaning; in both, legality of ''moonlighting'' is thus qualified with adjectives.<ref>Treffry, Diana (ed.). 1999. ''Collins Paperback English Dictionary.'' 4th ed. Glasgow: HarperCollins.</ref> <!--http://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/british/moonlight -->

